{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Left- and Right-Eye Image Separation.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Left- and Right-Eye Image Separation.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"left and right eye image separation left and right eye image separation","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Left- and Right-Eye Image Separation.md","fileName":"Left- and Right-Eye Image Separation.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Left- and Right-Eye Image Separation","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-15 18:17\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Stereoscopic Imaging and Conversion]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Stereoscopic Imaging and Conversion","Stereo Parallax and Convergence","digitalvisualeffectsandcompositing.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Left- and Right-Eye Image Separation.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Left- and Right-Eye Image Separation.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"left and right eye image separation left and right eye image separation","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Left- and Right-Eye Image Separation.md","fileName":"Left- and Right-Eye Image Separation.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Left- and Right-Eye Image Separation","heading":"Left- and Right-Eye Image Separation","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Stereoscopic imagery begins with two slightly different horizontal viewpoints of the same scene. The left view must reach the left eye and the right view the right eye; if both eyes receive an identical image, the intended binocular depth is absent. The separation can be provided by a physical barrier, color filters, or another display method. The principle remains the same despite the different devices used to present the views. The offset produces the [[Stereo Parallax and Convergence]] relationship that the viewer interprets as depth